course: dictionary
lesson: 631
title: One word, many meanings: back
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word back can change its meaning with its context. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. In «the back of the line», back means the last part, the farthest from the front. In «give it back», back means to where it was before.
explain: Back can also mean earlier in time, as in «three years back», or help and support, as in «to back someone». Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Back can be a noun, an adverb, an adjective or a verb. Casual and rude phrases like «back in a sec» or «get off my back» need the whole sentence to make sense, because the single word back does not tell you much on its own.
fact: Back can be a noun, an adverb, an adjective or a verb.
fact: «Give it back» means return it to the one who had it.
fact: «Back» can mean earlier in time, as in «three years back».
fact: «A setback» is a failure, like a small step back.
fact: «Talk behind someone's back» means gossip that the person does not hear.
exercise: «The children lined up at the back of the line.» What does back mean here? | Help or support | To where it was before | Your neck and bottom | *The last part, farthest from the front
exercise: «Please give it back when you are finished.» What does back mean here? | Earlier in time | Support | Stop bothering me | *To where it was before, to the one who had it
exercise: Maya says «back off» is a kind, helpful phrase. Which answer is right? | Back off means help and support | *Back off means stop bothering me | Back off means earlier in time | Back off means the last part of a line
test: «Three years back, the village had a flood.» What does back mean here? | The last part, farthest from the front | *Earlier in time | Help or support | To where it was before
test: In football and rugby, what is a back? | A referee who blows the whistle | *A player near your own goal | A word for a refund | A part of your body from your neck to your bottom
exam: «The Australian frilled lizard runs off on its back legs.» Which exam word could replace back here? | previously | *rear | refunded | returned
